-- 200_supergun_graphics_options.surql -- SUPERGUN Steam supershell GRAPHICS OPTIONS, 2026-09-07.
-- Three user-selectable options built and gated on branch gfx-options (worktree scratchpad/sg-gfx,
-- commits 247b881 / d9ea2e4 / 38cabda / e8af423 off c8b49f0): a GPU sprite scaler that scales the
-- INDEX (not the RGBA) in compute, sharp-bilinear edge sampling in the sprite PS, and a Lottes CRT
-- post-pass. RE METHOD: the design was derived first (gpu-index-scaler-design.md), the CPU functions
-- are the ORACLE, and every kernel is gated byte-exact against them before any timing was believed.
-- Forward slashes only in ref/note; no apostrophes.
USE NS re DB kb;

UPSERT source:supergun_gfx_options_gates_20260907 SET kind='reproduction', strength='reproduction',
  ref='supergun worktree scratchpad/sg-gfx branch gfx-options e8af423 off c8b49f0; runs rr_play/gfx_g0, rr_play/gfx_g0base, scratchpad/g_g1_dup4, g_g1h_k1, g_g1h_dup4, g_g2_s2x4, g_g2_mmpx4, g_m_*, g_n_*, g_s_*; report scratchpad/gfx-options-report.md.',
  note='Anchor mvc-live-skins-quarters/d3dcap/ttd/runs/vs-01/pre, arc Temp/sg_dis/game_50_afs.bin read-only, roster 14,23,19,20,22,21, window 1280x720 on a 240 Hz panel.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gpu_index_scaler_gated SET confidence='high', date='2026-09-07',
  contribution_candidate=true,
  title='SUPERGUN GPU sprite scaler: the k-times page is computed in INDEX space by compute shaders over the sidecar 1x PART canvas, and the three kernels equal their CPU oracles byte for byte on 22657 real pages',
  statement='Two Texture2DArray<R8_UNORM> of (32K)x(32K) x 2048, layer == slot exactly as the 1x atlas a_tex, one SRV + one UAV each; a 1024x1024 R8 scratch canvas holds the sidecar 1x whole-PART canvas and log2(K) ping-pong levels scale it; the tiles are then cut into their layers. draw_world_all binds the HD arrays at t0/t2 in place of a_tex and NOTHING else in the draw path changes -- psatlas and every sprite UV are untouched because the layer is (32K)x(32K) with the (K*T) head at the top-left, the same proportion as the 32x32 layer. Kernels cs_scale2x_level / cs_mmpx_level / cs_cut_tiles / cs_dup_tiles (cs_5_0) are literal ports of hd::scale2x / hd::mmpx2x / hd::page cut / hd::dup. GATES, all on the same 900-frame scripted window run: G0 the recorded-round pixel gate with every option Off == the base build 4435/4435 (rr_play/gfx_g0 vs gfx_g0base); G1a cs_dup_tiles == hd::page at SCALER 0 over 22657 slots at K=4, 0 mismatches (371 MB of index bytes read back and compared); G1b --hd 4 --hd-scaler dup back-buffer hashes == --hd 1, 900/900 identical; G2 cs_scale2x_level x2 + cs_cut_tiles == hd::scale2x x2 + the cut, 22657 slots 0 mismatches; G2 cs_mmpx_level x2 + cs_cut_tiles == hd::mmpx2x x2 + the cut, 22657 slots 0 mismatches. Every arm decoded exactly the same 22657 ring tiles / 14547 skipped / 1908 live slots as the untouched base.',
  consequence='The k-times work moves entirely off the CPU: textures bracket p99 0.48-0.73 ms against native 2.4 ms and against 21.87 ms for the 4x CPU sidecar the design measured; the dispatch block costs 0.3645/0.9492/1.5084 ms p50/p99/max of GPU time at MMPX 4x and 0.2314/0.4065/1.3793 at Scale2x 2x, asynchronous, with 0.10/0.37 ms of CPU share; VRAM 85 MB at K=4, 21 MB at K=2; peak process RSS unchanged at 323-324 MB. Zero PAD to SCANOUT over 33 ms after frame 120 in all 12 timing runs.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_sidecar_recorder_only SET confidence='high', date='2026-09-07',
  contribution_candidate=true,
  title='The k-times sprite path needs ONLY the two LZSS call redirects (H1 0x140612559 tiled, H2 0x140612342 bit-15); route R2 (the P1/P2/P3/P4 image patches) is not needed and was dropped',
  statement='hd.rs on branch gfx-options installs exactly two rel32 rewrites, both verified against the disassembly before any write (H1 E8 B2 1C 00 00, H2 E8 C9 1E 00 00) and both routed through a stub that preserves RCX/RDX/R8/R9/R10/R11 and 16-aligns the stack. Each handler decodes the part with the decodeA port, hands the game its EXACT 1x bytes, and records shell-side only: CANVASES (the whole 1x W x H part canvas, deduped by fnv64), TILED (ring slot -> canvas, col, row, T, fnv64 of the 1x T x T head), PARTS and UPLOAD_SLOT for the bit-15 path. The game therefore stays at exactly 1x -- the sim, the ring, DC-RAM and blk are byte-for-byte where they were, which is why G0 holds and why the ring tile counts are identical across every arm.',
  consequence='WHOLE-PART CONTEXT is the reason the canvas and not the 32x32 tile is the scaler input: a tile scaled alone clamps at its own edge and paints a false edge along every tile seam. It also means a change of scaler or of K is a pure render-side option -- no sim gate is put at risk by adding one.';

UPSERT finding:mmpx_index_space_label_equality SET confidence='high', date='2026-09-07',
  contribution_candidate=true,
  title='MMPX in INDEX space: every similar-colour test becomes label EQUALITY and the luma ordering tests read luma(palette[label]) = (r+g+b+1)*(256-a) from the draw own palette bank; two independent transliterations agree on 22657 pages',
  statement='The reference is the authors MMPX (McGuire and Gagiu, JCGT 2021) via the ITotalJustice C99 port, as transcribed in scratchpad/steam_samples.py mmpx2x. Ported twice and independently: hd::mmpx2x (Rust, the oracle) and cs_mmpx_level (HLSL cs_5_0). Both take the 16-entry RGBA palette of the DRAW -- the GPU reads row BANK of the 16x64 palette texture, the CPU reads main.rs pal_rgba(bank) -- and both compute luma with the reference formula, so label 0 (alpha 0 in pal_rgba, hence luma 256) is LIGHT exactly as a transparent pixel is in the reference. The output can only ever pick an EXISTING label, so no colour is invented and a skin is still just a palette. Border handling is CLAMP to the canvas. The two implementations produce byte-identical pages on 22657 real character pages, twice each at K=4.',
  consequence='The scaler output depends on (canvas bytes, palette bank), so the HD layer is re-cut when the tile generation, the bank palette signature, K or the scaler moves; the per-frame dispatch queue is keyed by (canvas id, bank). Measured in the output: MMPX 4x changes 0.434 percent of a 1280x720 frame and the distinct-colour count does NOT rise (163822 vs 163937 for Off).';

UPSERT finding:sharp_bilinear_seams_belong_to_the_slot_model SET confidence='high', date='2026-09-07',
  title='Sharp-bilinear on the Steam supershell sprite path shows tile seams and a dark fringe, and both belong to the 32x32 slot model rather than to any scaler -- they appear identically at K=1',
  statement='psatlas_sharp implements the capture expert model (steam_samples.py shade_indexed with filt sharp): the texel coordinate is snapped to the texel centre except for a ramp one OUTPUT PIXEL wide at the border (fwidth), the palette is applied PER TAP and the COLOURS are blended, never the indices, addressing CLAMP to match the point sampler. Two artifacts, both measured at --hd 1 as well as at --hd 4 (runs g_s_offsharp and g_s_mmpx4sharp, dumps hdshot_gfx_sharp_k1_zoom.png / hdshot_gfx_sharp_zoom.png): (1) TILE SEAMS -- a multi-tile part is several 32x32 quads meeting edge to edge, and under CLAMP the ramp at a quad edge re-reads the quad own edge texel instead of the neighbour, so a line runs along every tile boundary; (2) DARK FRINGE -- palette entry 0 is [0,0,0,0], so blending the COLOURS toward a transparent neighbour pulls RGB toward black as well as alpha toward 0; 13441 pixels in the fighter region are more than 30 of 765 darker than the point render at K=1.',
  consequence='Sharp-bilinear ships EXPERIMENTAL and the runner start-up line says so. The two fixes are known and cheap and not yet done: premultiply each tap RGB by its own alpha before the lerp and divide after (kills the fringe, cannot change an opaque interior pixel), and give each HD layer a one-texel gutter from the neighbouring tile of the same canvas (cs_cut_tiles already has col/row/T, so the gutter is free) with a UV inset.';

UPSERT finding:appshaderpackage_mfx_container_format SET confidence='high', date='2026-09-07',
  title='AppShaderPackage.mfx is a hashed-name container with ZERO DXBC chunks and no printable identifiers -- the Collection own filters A..G cannot be compiled or bound from the shell without the game own resource manager',
  statement='Measured first-hand on the users install, read-only: nativeDX11x64/app_shader/AppShaderPackage.mfx, 1960963 bytes. Header: +0x00 magic MFX NUL; +0x04 u16 53; +0x06 u16 36; +0x08 u32 0xB35F7274 (a name or tag hash); +0x0C u32 2912 = the entry count; +0x10 u32 0x001C8FDC = 1871836 (payload span); +0x18 u64 offset[2912], and 0x18 + 2912*8 = 0x5B18 == offset[0] exactly. Records are 0x58 / 0x68 / 0x78 bytes; the first is 01 .. 0e .. 8910 0001 ffffffff bcbcbcbc. The string DXBC occurs ZERO times in the whole file and there is not one printable string of length 5 or more in the first 192 KB, while byte entropy is 2.8 to 3.6 -- so the payload is structured, not encrypted, and the technique names are hashes. The names quoted by seed 198 (af_filter_lanczos and sharp, mask_line, ce_filter_lanczos, scan_*, dotMaskWeights, Crts*) are how the ENGINE asks for them; they could not be re-derived from mvc_dump.bin this pass (no lanczos / Crt / dotMask byte string in it -- probably UTF-16 or another module).',
  consequence='Combined with finding:native_render_shader_package (load via the game resource manager, route a), binding one of the Collection filters means bringing up the game own material and shader subsystem -- the nDraw host-graphics band that --host-gfx deliberately stands in for -- plus an MFX parser. Not reasonable effort for a display option. SUPERGUN ships its own Lottes pass instead, with an editable parameter block, and copies nothing from the install (BYOR intact).';

UPSERT finding:supergun_lottes_display_pass SET confidence='high', date='2026-09-07',
  title='SUPERGUN DISPLAY option: the world image goes to an offscreen RT the size of the back buffer and is resolved through one full-screen psdisplay pass; DISPLAY 0 takes no extra pass or target at all',
  statement='present() picks world_rtv = dp_rtv when DISPLAY != 0, draws the world pass and the game 2D blit into it, then resolves with a full-window quad through psdisplay into the back buffer; the shell menu and the diagnostics overlay are drawn AFTER the resolve, so UI text stays crisp. The pass is the capture expert block verbatim (steam-samples-report.md A2 / steam_samples.py crt_final): lin = pow(rgb, 2.2); s = (y + 0.5) * lines / H; d = s - (floor(s) + 0.5); beam = exp2(hardScan * d * d) with hardScan -8 and lines 224; aperture grille where output column x mod 3 gets maskLight 1.5 and the other two maskDark 0.5; out = pow(saturate(lin * beam * mask), 1/2.2). No resampling, no horizontal blur, no curvature, no bloom. Scanlines is the SAME pass with the mask forced to 1. Parameters are exposed in graphics.toml (crt_lines, crt_hardscan, crt_mask_dark, crt_mask_light, crt_gamma).',
  consequence='Cost is inside the noise: with MMPX 4x + CRT the present() GPU frame is 0.103/0.252/1.630 ms p50/p99/max against 0.097/0.366/1.025 with everything off, and zero PAD to SCANOUT over 33 ms. Measured effect on the frame: CRT changes 99.17 percent of pixels, Scanlines 77.79 percent.';

UPSERT finding:dxgi_scanout_join_is_a_noisy_instrument SET confidence='high', date='2026-09-07',
  title='The runner frames-scanned-out figure (LatFrame.sync joined by PresentCount) measures how far ahead the CPU is running, not whether frames were displayed -- do not gate on it',
  statement='On the same base binary, the same anchor and the same 900-frame script, the joined count was 412 of 900 free-running and 898 of 900 with --pixel-hash, whose per-frame back-buffer readback syncs the CPU to the GPU. Widening the join lookback from 8 to 240 frames did not fix it (PresentCount is the app own unique monotonic counter, so a wider window cannot mis-join), and across two full six-run batches the CONTROL arm alone spanned 99.4 percent and 45.8 percent with byte-identical flags while background machine load changed. stats fail was 2 to 4 and occluded 0 in every case, so it is not occlusion.',
  consequence='Gate 60 fps work on PAD to SCANOUT over 33 ms after frame 120 (zero in all 12 runs), the PAD to SCANOUT percentiles, the textures bracket and the GPU timestamps -- each against a control run in the SAME batch -- and report the join rate without gating on it. Belongs in INSTRUMENT-TRAPS.';

UPSERT finding:bit15_parts_still_never_drawn SET confidence='high', date='2026-09-07',
  title='Bit-15 (scale-walker) sprite records are still never DRAWN: 1068 are recorded by the sidecar every run and 0 reach the display list; the shell now counts and logs them',
  statement='Every sprite record with slot >= 0x390 that reaches the resolve loop is counted; the first sighting of each slot writes BIT-15 ON SCREEN frame N: slot 0x.. -- sidecar canvas KNOWN or unknown, and the run ends with WINDOW-GFX bit-15: N records drawn, M distinct slots. On the 900-frame scripted window run at MMPX 4x: 1068 bit-15 parts RECORDED by H2, 0 records drawn, 0 distinct slots. They are also not scaled by the GPU path (they are not 32x32 tiled slots and take the per-page RGBA route, exactly as native), so a bit-15 part on screen would render at 1x.',
  consequence='The open question needs a real play session with a hyper or a super. Until one produces a BIT-15 ON SCREEN line, teaching cs_cut_tiles about whole-image bit-15 canvases (hd::part_of and UPLOAD_SLOT are already wired) has no evidence behind it.';

RELATE finding:supergun_gpu_index_scaler_gated -> supports -> finding:supergun_sidecar_recorder_only SET date='2026-09-07';
RELATE finding:mmpx_index_space_label_equality -> supports -> finding:supergun_gpu_index_scaler_gated SET date='2026-09-07';
RELATE finding:appshaderpackage_mfx_container_format -> supports -> finding:supergun_lottes_display_pass SET date='2026-09-07';

